  data: {
    LLM: 'Game: Petteia. Two players move stones on a grid; custodian capture (flank on two sides). Win by capturing all opponent stones or blocking all moves. Pure strategy, no chance.',
    Player: "Petteia (also known as Poleis or 'Cities') is an ancient Greek board game of pure strategy and military logic. Featured in the writings of Plato and Aristotle, it is one of the world's first 'Pure Reason' games, containing no elements of chance. Players command 'Stones' on a grid, attempting to capture the opponent's forces by surrounding them from two sides (Custodian Capture). It is the direct ancestor of the Roman 'Ludus Latrunculorum' and is celebrated as a training tool for soldiers and philosophers alike.",
    objective: 'Capture all opponent stones or block the opponent so they have no legal moves.',
    gameplay: 'Two players take turns moving their stones on a square grid. Pieces move in all directions (orthogonally, one square or sliding like a rook). Capture is by custodian capture: when an opponent stone is flanked on two opposite sides by your stones, it is captured and removed. Win by capturing all opponent stones or blocking the opponent so they have no legal moves. No random elements; pure strategy.',
    keyRules: [
      'Pieces move orthogonally on the board, one square or sliding along a row or column.',
      'Capture by surrounding an opponent piece on two opposite sides (custodian capture).',
      'Win by capturing all opponent pieces or blocking all legal moves.',
      'Keep stones in formation; isolated pieces are vulnerable to pincer capture.',
    ],
